            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't really want to block this, but I don't see any provisions for change in upgrade in these config files.  Should we include at least some form of version.  I'm ok if it's in a short follow on patch.\n\nI'll reiterate that I'd really like to see this file be written as systemd units, so systemd is aware of the changes, we don't get mismatches, and things like systemctl disable still function.  I think I'm in the minority here, so that's fine."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "> I don't really want to block this, but I don't see any provisions for change in upgrade in these config files. Should we include at least some form of version.\n\nYeah, I was thinking \"this is so simple, it'll never change\" but we know how that works. I added a version.\n\n> I'll reiterate that I'd really like to see this file be written as systemd units, so systemd is aware of the changes, we don't get mismatches, and things like systemctl disable still function. I think I'm in the minority here, so that's fine.\n\nNoted, but yeah, that's not the design we're looking for and the compromise was a compile option. I wouldn't object to an addition later where this application monitored for systemd changes to the unit files and updated these persistent files."
                }
            ],
